00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:00.850
Let's move on.

42
00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:02.850
How about quantum-safe cryptography?

43
00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:04.850
In the world of data,

44
00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:07.850
there's not very much these days that travels out over a network

45
00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:10.850
or between entities without being encrypted at one end

46
00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:12.850
before being decrypted at the other.

47
00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:15.850
This is done by using a series of public and private keys.

48
00:02:15.850 --> 00:02:19.850
Those same keys are used to authenticate our communications.

49
00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:23.850
The entire public-private key ecosystem relies on two things.

50
00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:27.850
Those private keys remaining private and the encryption being so strong

51
00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:32.850
that even if someone did manage to intercept a few packets of data,

52
00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:34.850
they wouldn't be able to peek inside.

53
00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:36.850
Here's the problem.

54
00:02:36.850 --> 00:02:40.850
Today's sophisticated hackers are out there gathering that encrypted data,

55
00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:45.850
knowing that it may only be a few years before they get their hands on the tools to decrypt it.

56
00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:46.850
They're playing the long game here.

57
00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:49.850
And you may have heard figures about how it would take thousands

58
00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:52.850
or even millions of years to crack a properly encrypted key.

59
00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:56.850
But most of those figures are based on classical computing methods.

60
00:02:56.850 --> 00:03:00.850
Shores algorithm, a method for finding prime factors of an integer

61
00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:04.850
is a good example of something that runs very slowly on today's computers,

62
00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:09.850
but could be greatly accelerated once quantum computing advances in several areas.

63
00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:13.850
Long story short, the technology we consider ultra secure today

64
00:03:13.850 --> 00:03:16.850
could fall apart in the face of quantum computing,

65
00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:20.850
which is why quantum-safe cryptography on IBM Z provides not only

66
00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:25.850
algorithms and key exchange mechanisms with this emerging threat in mind.

67
00:03:25.850 --> 00:03:29.850
It also provides the tools to analyze your environment for potential exposures,

68
00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:32.850
facilitate the rollout of quantum-safe algorithms,

69
00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:36.850
and ensure keys are rotated and managed based on policy.

70
00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:40.850
Those tools include the ZOS encryption readiness toolkit,

71
00:03:40.850 --> 00:03:44.850
ZERT, application discovery and delivery intelligence,

72
00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:47.850
ADDI, and unified key orchestrator.

73
00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:50.850
There's also something you really ought to be aware of,

74
00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:54.850
which is still fairly new, called system recovery boost.

75
00:03:54.850 --> 00:03:56.850
This is a favorite of mine.

76
00:03:56.850 --> 00:03:58.850
So your typical mainframe is meant to be used.

77
00:03:58.850 --> 00:04:02.850
It's built with a capacity in mind, and any time it's not running applications,

78
00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:04.850
it's costing someone money.

79
00:04:04.850 --> 00:04:08.850
So it's important to keep those windows as brief as possible.

80
00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:12.850
Now, let me also say that most IBM Z environments

81
00:04:12.850 --> 00:04:16.850
will have multiple L-Pars across multiple physical systems and assist blocks,

82
00:04:16.850 --> 00:04:19.850
so they can do what's called a rolling IPL.

83
00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:24.850
So we're working on these two systems, and then these two systems,

84
00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:27.850
and when we work on these last two systems,

85
00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:32.850
everything's got maintenance, and we always had at least six systems ready to go.

86
00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:36.850
But even then, we don't want to spend all day doing these rolling IPLs,

87
00:04:36.850 --> 00:04:41.850
and we also want to spend as little time with a decreased pool of systems as possible.

88
00:04:41.850 --> 00:04:44.850
That's where system recovery boost comes in.

89
00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:48.850
During what is called the boost period, the system is allowed to use processors,

90
00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:52.850
which are typically off limits, reserved for specialty workloads,

91
00:04:52.850 --> 00:04:56.850
in order to get everything running at full capacity again as quickly as possible.

92
00:04:56.850 --> 00:04:59.850
That's the technology part of this.

93
00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:02.850
The other part that makes the people with the calculators happy,

94
00:05:02.850 --> 00:05:07.850
is that during the boost period, all that extra power doesn't increase software licensing costs.

95
00:05:07.850 --> 00:05:10.850
It's just there to help shorten that window.

96
